{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff -- 'content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md' 'content/seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md'\"","aggregated_output":"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-MhdrKEId' (errno=Operation not permitted)\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-tNxGj25D' (errno=Operation not permitted)\ndiff --git a/content/seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md b/content/seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\nindex 63bea2d6..32396601 100644\n--- a/content/seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\t\n+++ b/content/seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\t\n@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@ related:\n - **Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dep't (6th Cir. 2015)** — *split: open carry, without more, is not suspicion.* In an open-carry State, the mere sight of a lawfully carried firearm did not give reasonable suspicion to stop and disarm a pedestrian; officers who did so were denied [[Qualified Immunity|qualified immunity]]. 785 F.3d 1128. **Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.** [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2800431/shawn-northrup-v-city-of-toledo-police-dept/)\n - **United States v. Wilson (5th Cir. 2025)** — *post-Bruen: lawful gun possession alone is not suspicion.* After the Supreme Court's *Bruen* decision recognizing a general right to carry, the visible or suspected possession of a firearm cannot **by itself** ground reasonable suspicion, because public carry is now **presumptively lawful nationwide** rather than a state-by-state privilege; officers need particularized facts beyond the gun. No. 23-30777 (5th Cir. 2025). **Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.** [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10636220/united-states-v-wilson/)\n \n-The SCOTUS two-showing framework is stable, but the circuits divide over the **frisk branch** where a State permits gun carry: the Fourth Circuit [[Reading and Citing Cases#en-banc|en banc]] treats reasonable suspicion that a stopped person is *armed* as by itself enough to frisk (*[[United States v. Robinson|Robinson]]*), while the open-carry cases hold that lawfully carrying a firearm, without more, supplies neither suspicion of a crime nor a basis to disarm (*[[United States v. Black|Black]]*; *[[Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept|Northrup]]*). The dividing question is whether \"armed and dangerous\" collapses into \"armed\" once a State makes public carry lawful. After *Bruen*, the Fifth Circuit (*Wilson*) sharpens the open-carry side: because public carry is now presumptively lawful **nationwide**, the bare fact of a firearm supplies neither suspicion of a crime nor, without particularized danger facts, a basis to disarm.\n+The SCOTUS two-showing framework is stable, but the circuits divide over the **frisk branch** where a State permits gun carry: the Fourth Circuit [[Reading and Citing Cases#en-banc|en banc]] treats reasonable suspicion that a stopped person is *armed* as by itself enough to frisk (*[[United States v. Robinson|Robinson]]*), while the open-carry cases hold that lawfully carrying a firearm, without more, supplies neither suspicion of a crime nor a basis to disarm (*[[United States v. Black|Black]]*; *[[Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept|Northrup]]*). The dividing question is whether \"armed and dangerous\" collapses into \"armed\" once a State makes public carry lawful. After *Bruen*, the Fifth Circuit (*Wilson*) sharpens the open-carry side **on the stop branch**: because public carry is now presumptively lawful **nationwide**, the visible or suspected possession of a firearm cannot **by itself** ground reasonable suspicion for the stop; officers need particularized facts beyond the gun.\n \n <!-- United States v. Wilson (No. 23-30777, 5th Cir. 2025-07-17, cluster 10636220, lead 11102807): born-draft frontier-stub lake record added under RULING P4-11 (tripwire-arc ingestion) — the post-Bruen point on the frisk/RS branch. Bold plain-text brief-mention (no wikilink, no case page this phase); CL opinion link only. DISTINCT from the 9th Cir. hash-match United States v. Luke Wilson (cluster 5296785, cited on Private and Foreign Searches) — plain text avoids the [[United States v. Wilson]] wikilink which resolves to that 9th Cir. page. LINT-17: no page-less wikilink. -->\n \ndiff --git a/content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md b/content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\nindex 22e088a1..8a7d3522 100644\n--- a/content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\n+++ b/content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\n@@ -20,6 +20,10 @@ related:\n \n # The Exclusionary Rule\n \n+> [!rule] Black-letter rule\n+> The exclusionary rule bars the prosecution from using, in its **case-in-chief**, evidence obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment. *[[Weeks v. United States|Weeks]]*, 232 U.S. 383, [393](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/98094/weeks-v-united-states/) (1914); *[[Mapp v. Ohio|Mapp]]*, 367 U.S. 643, [655](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/106285/mapp-v-ohio/) (1961). The bar reaches the **fruits** of that violation as well, under the fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree doctrine developed on [[Fruits & Attenuation]]. It is **not a personal constitutional right** but a **judicially created remedy** whose primary justification is **deterring police misconduct**, so it is bounded by a cost-benefit test and does not reach settings where suppression would not deter — it does not apply to grand-jury proceedings, and its forum and cost-benefit boundaries are developed on [[The Good-Faith Exception]]. *[[United States v. Calandra|Calandra]]*, 414 U.S. 338, [348](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108898/united-states-v-calandra/) (1974).\n+> ^rule-exclusionary\n+\n When a Fourth Amendment violation produces evidence, the fight is rarely \"was there a violation\" alone. It is whether the remedy of **exclusion** actually follows, and it usually does not follow automatically. The exclusionary rule bars the prosecution from using, in its **case-in-chief**, evidence obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment and the **fruits** of that violation. It is **not a personal constitutional right** but a **judicially created remedy** whose primary modern justification is **deterring police misconduct**. Run the evidence through the sequence (**standing gate → fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree reach → escape hatches → cost-benefit boundaries**), and only if it survives all of them is it suppressed.\n \n This sub-umbrella holds the doctrine, with a page developing each escape hatch:\n@@ -32,7 +36,7 @@ This sub-umbrella holds the doctrine, with a page developing each escape hatch:\n \n **The threshold gate — standing.** Before any of this, suppression is available only to a defendant whose **own** Fourth Amendment rights were violated; rights are personal and may not be vicariously asserted. **No standing means no suppression**, even where officers plainly violated *someone's* rights. Standing is taught in full on **[[Standing to Challenge a Search]]**.\n \n-**Where the rule does not reach.** The same deterrence logic keeps the rule out of many settings. A [[Knock-and-Announce|knock-and-announce]] violation triggers no suppression ([[Knock-and-Announce|Hudson]]); the rule does not apply to grand-jury questioning, a federal civil tax proceeding, civil deportation, or parole revocation (the cost-benefit boundaries, developed with the deterrence rationale on [[The Good-Faith Exception]]); a violation of **state law** is not for that reason a Fourth Amendment violation, so it triggers no federal suppression (*[[Virginia v. Moore|Virginia v. Moore]]*, home [[Search Incident to Arrest]]). And suppression bars only the case-in-chief: illegally seized evidence may still impeach the defendant's own false testimony (the impeachment exception, on [[Fruits & Attenuation]]).\n+**Where the rule does not reach.** The same deterrence logic keeps the rule out of many settings. A [[Knock-and-Announce|knock-and-announce]] violation triggers no suppression ([[Knock-and-Announce|Hudson]]); the rule does not apply to grand-jury questioning, a federal civil tax proceeding, civil deportation, or parole revocation (*[[Pennsylvania Board of Probation and Parole v. Scott|Scott]]*; the cost-benefit boundaries, developed with the deterrence rationale on [[The Good-Faith Exception]]); a violation of **state law** is not for that reason a Fourth Amendment violation, so it triggers no federal suppression (*[[Virginia v. Moore|Virginia v. Moore]]*, home [[Search Incident to Arrest]]). And suppression bars only the case-in-chief: illegally seized evidence may still impeach the defendant's own false testimony (the impeachment exception, on [[Fruits & Attenuation]]).\n \n **The digital frontier.** Whether acquiring bulk digital data is a \"search\" at all is resolved by the Supreme Court in *[[Chatrie v. United States|Chatrie]]* (2026) (it is); how that search question plays out for geofence and location-history data is developed on [[The Third-Party Doctrine and Digital Surveillance]], and the good-faith fallback for pre-ruling warrants is on [[The Good-Faith Exception]].\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
