ls for the Armed Forces\\\", \\\"year\\\": 2025, \\\"citeCount\\\": 0, \\\"cites\\\": [], \\\"cluster_ids\\\": [10597640], \\\"n_anchors\\\": 8, \\\"anchors\\\": [\\\"Coolidge v. New Hampshire\\\", \\\"Georgia v. Randolph\\\", \\\"Herring v. United States\\\", \\\"Hudson v. Michigan\\\", \\\"United States v. Jacobsen\\\", \\\"United States v. Johns\\\", \\\"United States v. Place\\\", \\\"Walter v. United States\\\"], \\\"doctrines\\\": [\\\"searches\\\", \\\"seizures\\\", \\\"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing\\\", \\\"the-warrant\\\", \\\"warrant-exceptions\\\"], \\\"progeny_cache\\\": \\\"/Users/johngalt/cssi-lake/cache/progeny/coolidge-v-new-hampshire.jsonl\\\", \\\"cache_line\\\": 15}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/I2-GAP-candidates-all.jsonl-435-{\\\"case\\\": \\\"Jeffrey Bilyeu v. UT-Battelle, LLC\\\", \\\"tier\\\": \\\"low\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"candidate\\\", \\\"court_class\\\": \\\"circuit\\\", \\\"court\\\": \\\"Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\\\", \\\"year\\\": 2025, \\\"citeCount\\\": 0, \\\"cites\\\": [], \\\"cluster_ids\\\": [10679677], \\\"n_anchors\\\": 1, \\\"anchors\\\": [\\\"Corley v. United States\\\"], \\\"doctrines\\\": [\\\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment\\\"], \\\"progeny_cache\\\": \\\"/Users/johngalt/cssi-lake/cache/progeny/corley-v-united-states.jsonl\\\", \\\"cache_line\\\": 2}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/I2-GAP-candidates-all.jsonl-436-{\\\"case\\\": \\\"United States v. Fishman, Giannelli\\\", \\\"tier\\\": \\\"low\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"candidate\\\", \\\"court_class\\\": \\\"circuit\\\", \\\"court\\\": \\\"Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit\\\", \\\"year\\\": 2025, \\\"citeCount\\\": 0, \\\"cites\\\": [], \\\"cluster_ids\\\": [10675408], \\\"n_anchors\\\": 1, \\\"anchors\\\": [\\\"Corley v. United States\\\"], \\\"doctrines\\\": [\\\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment\\\"], \\\"progeny_cache\\\": \\\"/Users/johngalt/cssi-lake/cache/progeny/corley-v-united-states.jsonl\\\", \\\"cache_line\\\": 3}\\n--\\n_run/s9/p4/out/COH-findings.jsonl-59-{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.candidate.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"class\\\": \\\"callout-registry-no-callout\\\", \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\\\", \\\"line\\\": 1, \\\"assertion_id\\\": \\\"d589d53c354f93cf\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"registry node 'remedy.exclusionary' carries a statement but its home_page (type=doctrine) has NO [!rule] callout to deep-equal against (S5 R2/R10a)\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"registry_node=remedy.exclusionary | registry_file=_overhaul2/points/registry.yaml:583 | home_page=content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md has no '> [!rule]' block | STATEMENT: The exclusionary rule bars the prosecution from using, in its case-in-chief, evidence obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment and the fruits of that violation; it is not a personal constitutional right but a judicially created remedy whose primary justification is deterring police misconduct. *Weeks v. United States* (1914); *Mapp v. Ohio* (1961); *United States v. Calandra*, 414 U.S. 338 (1974).\\\", \\\"needs_cl\\\": false, \\\"lane\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-4-8\\\"}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/COH-findings.jsonl-60-{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.candidate.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"class\\\": \\\"callout-registry-cite-divergence\\\", \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/Fruits and Attenuation.md\\\", \\\"line\\\": 1, \\\"assertion_id\\\": \\\"b67489c3ee31d31c\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"registry statement for 'remedy.fruits-attenuation' is NOT deep-equal to its home-page callout: citation tokens diverge (pincite_drop) (S5 R2/R10a)\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"registry_node=remedy.fruits-attenuation | subclass=pincite_drop | callout_cites=['371U.S.471,487-88', '422U.S.590,603-04'] stmt_cites=['371U.S.471', '422U.S.590'] | callout_years=['1963', '1975'] stmt_years=['1963', '1975'] || CALLOUT(norm): Suppression reaches not only the evidence seized in the unlawful act but the derivative evidence it produces, the \\\\\\\"fruit of the poisonous tree.\\\\\\\" The reach is not but-for causation: the question is \\\\\\\"whether, granting establishment of the primary illegality, the evidence to which instant objection is made has been come at by exploitation of that illegality or instead by means sufficiently distinguishable to be purged of the primary taint.\\\\\\\" Wong Sun v. United States, 371 U.S. 471, 487-88 (1963). Taint is purged by attenuation when the connection between the illegality and the evidence becomes so weakened that deterrence is no longer served, judged by temporal proximity, intervening circumstances, and, most important, the purpose and flagrancy of the misconduct. Brown v. Illinois, 422 U.S. 590, 603-04 (1975). || STATEMENT(norm): Suppression reaches the derivative evidence produced by a Fourth Amendment violation (the fruit of the poisonous tree), but not on but-for causation; the question is whether the evidence was come at by exploitation of the illegality or by means sufficiently distinguishable to be purged of the taint, and the taint is purged by attenuation judged by temporal proximity, intervening circumstances, and the purpose and flagrancy of the misconduct. Wong Sun v. United States, 371 U.S. 471 (1963); Brown v. Illinois, 422 U.S. 590 (1975).\\\", \\\"needs_cl\\\": false, \\\"lane\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-4-8\\\"}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/COH-findings.jsonl:61:{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.candidate.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"class\\\": \\\"callout-registry-cite-divergence\\\", \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/The Good-Faith Exception.md\\\", \\\"line\\\": 1, \\\"assertion_id\\\": \\\"e6ae67af2da706bb\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"registry statement for 'remedy.good-faith' is NOT deep-equal to its home-page callout: citation tokens diverge (different_authority) (S5 R2/R10a)\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"registry_node=remedy.good-faith | subclass=different_authority | callout_cites=['468U.S.897,922'] stmt_cites=['468U.S.897', '555U.S.135'] | callout_years=['1984'] stmt_years=['1984', '2009'] || CALLOUT(norm): Because the exclusionary rule is a deterrent remedy and not a personal right, suppression is unwarranted where officers acted in objectively reasonable reliance on an authority they were entitled to trust and that was only later found invalid; excluding that evidence would deter nothing. United States v. Leon, 468 U.S. 897, 922 (1984). Good faith fails, and suppression follows, in United States v. Leon's four situations: (1) a knowing or reckless false affidavit (Franks v. Delaware); (2) a magistrate who wholly abandoned the neutral-and-detached role; (3) a bare-bones affidavit \\\\\\\"so lacking in indicia of probable cause\\\\\\\" that belief in it is unreasonable; and (4) a facially deficient / general warrant no officer could reasonably presume valid. 468 U.S. at 923. || STATEMENT(norm): Because the exclusionary rule is a deterrent remedy and not a personal right, suppression is unwarranted where officers acted in objectively reasonable reliance on a warrant, statute, ordinance, or record later found invalid; good faith fails, and suppression follows, in Leon's four situations (a knowing or reckless false affidavit, a magistrate who abandoned the neutral role, a bare-bones affidavit, or a facially deficient warrant). United States v. Leon, 468 U.S. 897 (1984); Herring v. United States, 555 U.S. 135 (2009).\\\", \\\"needs_cl\\\": false, \\\"lane\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-4-8\\\"}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/COH-findings.jsonl-62-{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.candidate.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"class\\\": \\\"callout-registry-cite-divergence\\\", \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/Inevitable Discovery and Independent Source.md\\\", \\\"line\\\": 1, \\\"assertion_id\\\": \\\"3e9f0aceccfd9058\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"registry statement for 'remedy.inevitable-independent' is NOT deep-equal to its home-page callout: citation tokens diverge (pincite_drop) (S5 R2/R10a)\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"registry_node=remedy.inevitable-independent | subclass=pincite_drop | callout_cites=['467U.S.431,444', '487U.S.533,542'] stmt_cites=['467U.S.431', '487U.S.533'] | callout_years=['1984', '1988'] stmt_years=['1984', '1988'] || CALLOUT(norm): Independent source admits evidence that was in fact also obtained through a lawful source genuinely independent of the illegality: admissible if \\\\\\\"the search pursuant to warrant was in fact a genuinely independent source,\\\\\\\" but not if the decision to seek the warrant was \\\\\\\"prompted by what they had seen during the initial entry.\\\\\\\" Murray v. United States, 487 U.S. 533, 542 (1988). Inevitable discovery admits evidence that would have been found anyway: admissible if the prosecution \\\\\\\"establish[es] by a preponderance of the evidence that the information ultimately or inevitably would have been discovered by lawful means.\\\\\\\" Nix v. Williams, 467 U.S. 431, 444 (1984). || STATEMENT(norm): Independent source admits evidence that was in fact also obtained through a lawful source genuinely independent of the illegality and not prompted by it; inevitable discovery admits evidence that would ultimately or inevitably have been discovered by lawful means, proved by a preponderance of the evidence. Murray v. United States, 487 U.S. 533 (1988); Nix v. Williams, 467 U.S. 431 (1984).\\\", \\\"needs_cl\\\": false, \\\"lane\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-4-8\\\"}\\n_run/s9/p4/out/COH-findings.jsonl-63-{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.candidate.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"class\\\": \\\"callout-registry-cite-divergence\\\", \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"content/the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\\\", \\\"line\\\": 1, \\\"assertion_id\\\": \\\"2a175fd27c04eeb8\\\", \\\"claim\\\": \\\"registry statement for 'remedy.standing' is NOT deep-equal to its home-page callout: citation tokens diverge (different_authority) (S5 R2/R10a)\\\", \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"registry_node=remedy.standing | subclass=different_authority | callout_cites=['439U.S.128,133-34'] stmt_cites=['394U.S.165', '439U.S.128'] | callout_years=['1978'] stmt_years=['1969', '1978'] || CALLOUT(norm): Fourth Amendment rights are personal and \\\\\\\"may not be vicariously asserted.\\\\\\\" A defendant may move to suppress only if the search or seizure infringed his own legitimate expectation of privacy in the place or thing searched, measured by the Katz v. United States test. Rakas v. Illinois, 439 U.S. 128, 133-34, 143 (1978). \\\\\\\"Standing\\\\\\\" is not a separate doctrine; it is this merits question. No standing means no suppression, even where officers plainly violated someone else's rights. || STATEMENT(norm): Fourth Amendment rights are personal and may not be vicariously asserted; a defendant may seek suppression only if he had a personal, legitimate expectation of privacy - measured by the Katz test - in the place or thing searched. Rakas v. Illinois, 439 U.S. 128 (1978); Alderman v. United States, 394 U.S. 165 (1969).\\\", \\\"needs_cl\\\": false, \\\"lane\\\": \\\"COH\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"claude-opus-4-8\\\"}\\n--\\n_run/s9/p4/out/PAIR-P4-dispositions.jsonl-35-{\\\"row\\\": \\\"p4.pair.v1\\\", \\\"ws\\\": \\\"PAIR\\\", \\\"packet\\\": \\\"PAIR-P4\\\", \\\"pair_id\\\": \\\"PAIR-0365\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"shared-case\\\", \\\"page_a\\\": \\\"content/searches/two-definitions-of-search/Trespass.